1. Have headers been fully parsed?

Check if you have reached:

\r\n\r\n (end of headers)

If NO:

- Request is incomplete
- Client closed early

✔ Action:

return 400 Bad Request
    or silently drop connection (depending on server policy)

2. Is the HTTP request line valid and complete?

Ensure you have:

Method (GET/POST/etc.)
Path
Version (HTTP/1.0 or 1.1)
If missing/partial:

✔ Action:

400 Bad Request

3. Are you expecting a request body?

Determine from headers:

Check:
    Content-Length present?
Transfer-Encoding: chunked?
    
Case A: No body expected (GET, HEAD, etc.)

If headers are complete and method has no body:

✔ EOF is OK
✔ Action:

treat request as complete
process immediately
Case B: Content-Length exists

Check:

bytes_received_body < Content-Length
If YES:

✔ Problem: incomplete body

✔ Action:

400 Bad Request or 408 Request Timeout
Case C: Chunked transfer encoding

Check chunk state:

Have all chunks fully parsed?
Have you seen terminating 0\r\n\r\n chunk?
If NO:

✔ EOF means truncated stream

✔ Action:

400 Bad Request
Case D: HTTP/1.0 with no Content-Length

This is special:

Body = “until connection close”

So EOF is:

✔ normal end of body

✔ Action:

finalize request body
proceed to handler
4. Are you mid-request parsing state?

Check your parser state machine:

States where EOF is BAD:
reading headers
reading partial body (Content-Length or chunked)
reading chunk metadata

✔ Action:

treat as incomplete request
State where EOF is OK:
request fully parsed
waiting for next request (keep-alive idle)

✔ Action:

close connection cleanly
5. Did you already finish a full request on this connection?

Important for pipelining (HTTP/1.1):

You may have:

[Request 1 complete][Request 2 partial]

If EOF occurs:

✔ If next request not fully parsed:

incomplete → error or discard

✔ If everything is clean:

normal close
6. Connection context checks

Check socket-level context:

Was Connection: close sent?
Is keep-alive expected?
Are there leftover buffered bytes?
7. Protocol version handling
HTTP/1.0
No persistent connection by default
EOF often = request end (if no Content-Length)
HTTP/1.1
Persistent connection default
EOF is usually unexpected unless:
request complete
server initiated close
client explicitly closed
8. Error classification decision tree

When read() == 0:

Step 1: headers complete?
No → 400
Step 2: body expected?
Yes → check completeness
Step 3: body complete?
No → 400 / 408
Step 4: everything complete?
Yes → clean close
9. Minimal rule set (practical implementation)

You can simplify into:

if headers_not_complete:
    reject (400)

else if body_expected and body_not_complete:
    reject (400 or 408)

else:
    if request_complete:
        accept + close
    else if HTTP/1.0 and no Content-Length:
        finalize using EOF
    else:
        treat as connection abort